The attached image illustrates the local inflammatory response two weeks post-injection of either aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant (left panel) or Nano-11 (right panel) with ovalbumin, as analyzed by H&E staining. The left panel, representing AH, shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (indicated by asterisks) with dense aggregates of macrophages and scattered eosinophils within the skeletal muscle (M). In contrast, the right panel, representing Nano-11, exhibits significantly less residual inflammation (also indicated by an asterisk), characterized by relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords between muscle fibers. This demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a more transient and less severe inflammatory reaction compared to the persistent granulomatous inflammation caused by aluminum adjuvants, supporting its excellent safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.